{
 "id": "2023-zhang-flower",
 "name": "Flower Colour: Preference, Uplift and Relaxation",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the online questionnaire in Zhang, L., Dempsey, N., & Cameron, R. W. F. (2023). Flowers – Sunshine for the soul! How does floral colour influence preference, feelings of relaxation and positive up-lift? Urban Forestry & Urban Greening 79:127795. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ufug.2022.127795\n\nParticipants see the paper's eight photomontages of a meadow-style planting of 'daisy-like' flowers (Figure 1, labelled A–H: blue, purple, white, orange, red, pink, yellow, brown), which differ only in flower colour. They pick the plant community they like best, rank the eight from most to least uplifting and from most to least relaxing (rank 1 = 8 points down to 1; pictures left unranked score 1, as in the paper), say why after each, and answer the paper's profile questions (age band, gender, urban/suburban/rural, liking of plants and of flower gardens, gardening frequency, connection to nature).\n\nOriginal study: 670 UK adults online (SmartSurvey, August–November 2020). White compositions were liked best (26%), then blue (18%) and orange (16%); orange was most often named the most uplifting; age was the only demographic factor linked to colour preference, and people tended to find their own preferred colour the most relaxing.\n\nData: like_best = { choice, shown_order }; uplifting and relaxing = { ranking, scores (colour → 1–8), no_opinion, shown_order }; ranking[0] is the \"most uplifting/relaxing\" pick. Analyse with percentages of each colour chosen and chi-square / Fisher's exact tests against the profile variables, as in the paper.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the paper's Appendix A questionnaire was not available to us, so all wording except the three quoted questions and \"Can you say why?\" is ours, and the answer options follow the paper's Table 1; (2) the paper asked separately for the most uplifting/relaxing composition and for a ranking; here the first picture in each ranking is that choice; (3) the Perceived Stress Scale questions are left out (add your own licensed copy if you need them), and nature relatedness is a single self-rating question rather than the paper's measure; (4) participants may tick \"I have no opinion\" for uplift and relaxation, since the paper reports fewer respondents for those questions.\n\nImages: Figure 1 of the open-access article (CC BY-NC-ND 4.0), cropped into the eight panels, shown with credit for non-commercial research; the template is removed if the authors object.",
